-- ===== Retroactive knowledge box: q_sba_gout_diagnosis_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p>This vignette describes a gouty tophus with likely acute-on-chronic gout, in a patient with classic risk factors (alcohol excess, metabolic syndrome/T2DM, cardiovascular disease, and recent physiological stress from hospital admission). The chalky, irregular, firm lump is characteristic of a tophus. While NICE guidance suggests measuring urate levels to support diagnosis, this question asks for the <strong>best diagnostic modality</strong> — which is synovial fluid aspiration demonstrating monosodium urate crystals.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> ESR is a nonspecific inflammatory marker that may be raised in acute gout (or many other inflammatory/infective conditions), but does not confirm the diagnosis or distinguish gout from other causes such as septic arthritis or pseudogout.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Skin biopsy could histologically demonstrate urate crystal deposition within a tophus, but this is more invasive, slower, and less specific/practical than joint aspiration; it is not the standard first-line diagnostic test.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> The gold-standard investigation for gout is aspiration of joint/tophus fluid with polarised light microscopy, demonstrating needle-shaped, negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals. This also allows exclusion of septic arthritis via Gram stain and culture — a crucial differential given the hot, firm, tender presentation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> NICE NG219 suggests measuring serum urate to confirm the diagnosis, and this would be the correct answer if the question asked for the best <em>next step</em> rather than the best diagnostic <em>modality</em>, which is synovial fluid analysis.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Toe X-ray may show established changes of chronic tophaceous gout (juxta-articular erosions with overhanging edges) but is neither sensitive nor specific for an acute presentation and does not provide a definitive diagnosis.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Gout</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Diagnosis</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Clinical presentation</th><td>Acute, severe <strong>monoarticular</strong> pain, swelling, erythema and warmth &mdash; classically the <strong>first metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint</strong></td></tr><tr><th>Definitive diagnosis</th><td>Joint aspiration with <strong>polarised light microscopy</strong>: needle-shaped, <strong>negatively birefringent</strong> monosodium urate crystals</td></tr><tr><th>Excluding sepsis</th><td>The aspirate is also sent for Gram stain and culture to exclude <strong>septic arthritis</strong></td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Management of Acute Gout</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>First-line (equal options)</th><td>An <strong>NSAID</strong>, <strong>colchicine</strong>, or a <strong>short course of oral corticosteroid</strong> &mdash; chosen according to comorbidities, co-prescriptions and patient preference (oral corticosteroids are used <em>off-label</em> for this indication)</td></tr><tr><th>If taking an NSAID</th><td>Consider adding a <strong>proton pump inhibitor (PPI)</strong></td></tr><tr><th>If NSAIDs and colchicine are contraindicated, not tolerated or ineffective</th><td>Consider an <strong>intra-articular or intramuscular corticosteroid injection</strong> (off-label)</td></tr><tr><th>Adjunct</th><td><strong>Ice packs</strong> applied to the joint, alongside prescribed medicine, may help relieve pain</td></tr></table><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>First-line option</th><th>Favour when</th><th>Avoid / caution when</th></tr><tr><td><strong>NSAID</strong></td><td>No cardiovascular, renal or GI contraindications</td><td>Ischaemic heart disease, heart failure, renal impairment, peptic ulcer disease, anticoagulation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Colchicine</strong></td><td>NSAIDs contraindicated (e.g. cardiovascular disease)</td><td>Significant renal/hepatic impairment; interacting P-gp/CYP3A4 inhibitors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Oral corticosteroid</strong></td><td>NSAIDs and colchicine both unsuitable</td><td>Poorly controlled diabetes; infection not yet excluded</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Colchicine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Main side effect</th><td><strong>Dose-dependent gastrointestinal upset</strong> (diarrhoea, nausea, vomiting) &mdash; often the limiting factor for tolerability</td></tr><tr><th>Dose adjustment</th><td>Requires <strong>dose reduction in renal and hepatic impairment</strong></td></tr><tr><th>Drug interactions</th><td><strong>P-glycoprotein and CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g. clarithromycin, ciclosporin, some antifungals) can significantly increase colchicine levels and toxicity risk</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Relevant in patients with atrial fibrillation: verapamil, diltiazem and amiodarone are also P-glycoprotein/CYP3A4 inhibitors, so check the drug history and reduce the colchicine dose if they are co-prescribed.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">What NOT to Do During an Acute Flare</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Do <strong>not start</strong> urate-lowering therapy (allopurinol, febuxostat) during an acute attack</td></tr><tr><td>Do <strong>not stop</strong> urate-lowering therapy if already established &mdash; continue it alongside acute treatment</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Long-term / Prophylactic Management (Once the Flare Has Settled)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Allopurinol</strong> (first-line) or <strong>febuxostat</strong>, titrated to a target serum urate</td></tr><tr><td>Lifestyle modification: weight loss, reduced alcohol and purine intake, adequate hydration</td></tr><tr><td>Cover with <strong>low-dose colchicine or an NSAID</strong> when initiating urate-lowering therapy to reduce the risk of flares</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"nice-guideline-btn\" onclick=\"openGuidelineModal(''ng219'',''NICE NG219 — Gout'')\">\n  <svg width=\"15\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z\"/><polyline points=\"14 2 14 8 20 8\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"17\"/></svg>\n  NICE NG219 &mdash; Gout Summary\n</button></div></div>\n</div></div>","relevantReading":[{"label":"Gout: diagnosis and management (NG219)","source":"NICE","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng219"},{"label":"Gout","source":"GPnotebook","url":"https://gpnotebook.com/en-GB/pages/rheumatology/gout"}]}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_sba_gout_diagnosis_001';

-- ===== Retroactive knowledge box: q_sba_gout_flare_treatment_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p>Acute gout flares are driven by an intense inflammatory response to monosodium urate crystal deposition. First-line management centres on rapid, effective anti-inflammatory treatment — NSAIDs (including COX-2 selective agents), colchicine, or a short course of oral corticosteroids — chosen according to comorbidities and contraindications.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Paracetamol is a simple analgesic with no significant anti-inflammatory action; it is inadequate as monotherapy for the intense inflammatory pain of an acute gout flare.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Opioid analgesia addresses pain but does nothing to treat the underlying crystal-induced inflammation, and carries unnecessary dependence/side-effect risk when effective anti-inflammatory options exist and are not contraindicated.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> Etoricoxib is a COX-2 selective NSAID specifically licensed for acute gout, providing effective anti-inflammatory analgesia. In a patient without renal, cardiovascular, or GI contraindications, an oral NSAID/COX-2 inhibitor is considered first-line treatment.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Topical NSAID gel provides insufficient systemic anti-inflammatory effect for an acute, intensely inflamed joint; it is better suited to superficial musculoskeletal pain than true crystal arthropathy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Intra-articular steroid injection is effective, particularly for monoarticular flares or when oral options are contraindicated, but requires exclusion of septic arthritis first and is generally reserved for cases where oral therapy is unsuitable or the diagnosis is already confirmed — making it not the best first-line choice over an oral NSAID in this straightforward scenario.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Gout</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Diagnosis</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Clinical presentation</th><td>Acute, severe <strong>monoarticular</strong> pain, swelling, erythema and warmth &mdash; classically the <strong>first metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint</strong></td></tr><tr><th>Definitive diagnosis</th><td>Joint aspiration with <strong>polarised light microscopy</strong>: needle-shaped, <strong>negatively birefringent</strong> monosodium urate crystals</td></tr><tr><th>Excluding sepsis</th><td>The aspirate is also sent for Gram stain and culture to exclude <strong>septic arthritis</strong></td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Management of Acute Gout</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>First-line (equal options)</th><td>An <strong>NSAID</strong>, <strong>colchicine</strong>, or a <strong>short course of oral corticosteroid</strong> &mdash; chosen according to comorbidities, co-prescriptions and patient preference (oral corticosteroids are used <em>off-label</em> for this indication)</td></tr><tr><th>If taking an NSAID</th><td>Consider adding a <strong>proton pump inhibitor (PPI)</strong></td></tr><tr><th>If NSAIDs and colchicine are contraindicated, not tolerated or ineffective</th><td>Consider an <strong>intra-articular or intramuscular corticosteroid injection</strong> (off-label)</td></tr><tr><th>Adjunct</th><td><strong>Ice packs</strong> applied to the joint, alongside prescribed medicine, may help relieve pain</td></tr></table><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>First-line option</th><th>Favour when</th><th>Avoid / caution when</th></tr><tr><td><strong>NSAID</strong></td><td>No cardiovascular, renal or GI contraindications</td><td>Ischaemic heart disease, heart failure, renal impairment, peptic ulcer disease, anticoagulation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Colchicine</strong></td><td>NSAIDs contraindicated (e.g. cardiovascular disease)</td><td>Significant renal/hepatic impairment; interacting P-gp/CYP3A4 inhibitors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Oral corticosteroid</strong></td><td>NSAIDs and colchicine both unsuitable</td><td>Poorly controlled diabetes; infection not yet excluded</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Colchicine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Main side effect</th><td><strong>Dose-dependent gastrointestinal upset</strong> (diarrhoea, nausea, vomiting) &mdash; often the limiting factor for tolerability</td></tr><tr><th>Dose adjustment</th><td>Requires <strong>dose reduction in renal and hepatic impairment</strong></td></tr><tr><th>Drug interactions</th><td><strong>P-glycoprotein and CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g. clarithromycin, ciclosporin, some antifungals) can significantly increase colchicine levels and toxicity risk</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Relevant in patients with atrial fibrillation: verapamil, diltiazem and amiodarone are also P-glycoprotein/CYP3A4 inhibitors, so check the drug history and reduce the colchicine dose if they are co-prescribed.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">What NOT to Do During an Acute Flare</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Do <strong>not start</strong> urate-lowering therapy (allopurinol, febuxostat) during an acute attack</td></tr><tr><td>Do <strong>not stop</strong> urate-lowering therapy if already established &mdash; continue it alongside acute treatment</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Long-term / Prophylactic Management (Once the Flare Has Settled)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Allopurinol</strong> (first-line) or <strong>febuxostat</strong>, titrated to a target serum urate</td></tr><tr><td>Lifestyle modification: weight loss, reduced alcohol and purine intake, adequate hydration</td></tr><tr><td>Cover with <strong>low-dose colchicine or an NSAID</strong> when initiating urate-lowering therapy to reduce the risk of flares</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"nice-guideline-btn\" onclick=\"openGuidelineModal(''ng219'',''NICE NG219 — Gout'')\">\n  <svg width=\"15\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z\"/><polyline points=\"14 2 14 8 20 8\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"17\"/></svg>\n  NICE NG219 &mdash; Gout Summary\n</button></div></div>\n</div></div>","relevantReading":[{"label":"Gout: diagnosis and management (NG219)","source":"NICE","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng219"},{"label":"Gout","source":"GPnotebook","url":"https://gpnotebook.com/en-GB/pages/rheumatology/gout"}]}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_sba_gout_flare_treatment_001';

-- ===== Retroactive knowledge box: q_gabapentin_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p>Gabapentin and pregabalin are α2-δ ligands that modulate voltage-gated calcium channels at presynaptic terminals, reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release. Despite their names, they do not act at GABA receptors. Their pharmacokinetics differ importantly, especially in terms of absorption.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Gabapentin has variable and dose-dependent oral bioavailability of approximately 27–60%, whereas pregabalin has linear, higher bioavailability of ~90%. This is a key pharmacokinetic difference between the two drugs.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Despite being named after GABA, gabapentin does not bind to GABA-A or GABA-B receptors. It binds to the α2-δ subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels. Confusing it with a GABAergic agent is a classic examination trap.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — Gabapentin is absorbed via the LAT1 (large neutral amino acid transporter) in the small intestine. This is a saturable system, which is why bioavailability decreases at higher doses — a clinically relevant fact.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — Gabapentin typically reaches peak plasma concentration at approximately 2–3 hours after oral dosing, not 30 minutes. The 30-minute figure is more consistent with rapidly absorbed agents like immediate-release opioids.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Because of the saturable transporter mechanism, as the oral dose increases, the fraction absorbed decreases. This means higher single doses are proportionally less effective — a key reason for TDS rather than once-daily dosing.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Gabapentinoids</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification and Mechanism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Structurally related to GABA, but <strong>no direct action at GABA receptors</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Both bind to the <strong>alpha-2-delta (&alpha;2&delta;) subunit of presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels</strong>, reducing calcium influx and the subsequent release of excitatory neurotransmitters (glutamate, noradrenaline, substance P) from presynaptic nerve terminals</td></tr><tr><td>Net effect: reduced neuronal excitability &mdash; anti-epileptic, analgesic and anxiolytic effects</td></tr></table><div style=\"text-align:center;margin-top:12px\"><img src=\"images/gabapentinoid-a2d-mechanism.svg\" alt=\"Gabapentin and pregabalin bind the alpha-2-delta subunit of presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing calcium influx and release of glutamate, noradrenaline and substance P, with no direct action at GABA receptors\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:4px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Pharmacokinetics</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Gabapentin</th><th>Pregabalin</th></tr><tr><td>Hepatic metabolism</td><td>None</td><td>Negligible</td></tr><tr><td>Renal excretion</td><td>Almost entirely unchanged</td><td>Almost entirely unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Half-life</td><td>5&ndash;7 hours</td><td>5&ndash;7 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Absorption / bioavailability</td><td><strong>Non-linear</strong> &mdash; saturable absorption via an L-amino acid transporter, so bioavailability falls as the dose rises</td><td><strong>Linear</strong> &mdash; dose-proportional plasma concentration</td></tr><tr><td>Typical dosing</td><td>Three times daily</td><td>Twice daily</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Both depend on renal excretion, so doses must be reduced in renal impairment.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Efficacy and Harm (Gabapentin)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td><strong>NNT</strong></td><td>&ge;50% pain reduction in chronic neuropathic pain (overall)</td><td>~6&ndash;8</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NNH</strong></td><td>Minor side effects</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NNH</strong></td><td>Major side effects</td><td>12</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Uses</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Licensed in the UK</th><td>Gabapentin and pregabalin are both licensed for neuropathic pain; gabapentin is also licensed for partial seizures and postherpetic neuralgia specifically</td></tr><tr><th>Neuropathic pain</th><td>Painful diabetic neuropathy, postherpetic neuralgia, and other peripheral/central neuropathic pain</td></tr><tr><th>Pregabalin only</th><td>Additionally licensed for <strong>generalised anxiety disorder</strong> and as an adjunct for focal seizures</td></tr><tr><th>Perioperative use</th><td>Gabapentinoids reduce postoperative pain and opioid requirements, and reduce the incidence of vomiting, pruritus and urinary retention &mdash; but <strong>increase the risk of sedation</strong></td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Common Side Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Side effect</th></tr><tr><td>Dizziness, somnolence/sedation, ataxia</td></tr><tr><td>Peripheral oedema, weight gain</td></tr><tr><td>Dry mouth, blurred vision</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Dependence and Misuse Potential</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Both <strong>reclassified as Class C, Schedule 3 controlled drugs</strong> in the UK (April 2019) because of recognised dependence and misuse potential</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawal symptoms (insomnia, headache, nausea, anxiety, sweating) can occur on abrupt discontinuation &mdash; <strong>taper gradually</strong></td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_gabapentin_001';
